What I want to do in the code that I highlighted above with the Debug.Assert line is, in case LastIndexOf (or any another method) returns a value greater or equal to 0, I need to ensure that the original 'arrList' and new list 'arrList.LastIndexOf(x)' have the same size (i.e., they have the same count of items). The assertion PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, "Count is changed"); will do that. Also, when LastIndexOf returns a value greater or equal than 0, I want to verify whether the 'arrList.Contains(x)' is preserved.

A: In your comment to the other answer, you explained what you want, and now I'm certain I understood correctly. The test should indeed verify that if LastIndexOf returns a value >= 0, the size of the collection stays the same and Contains is preserved.

The PexAssert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, "Count is changed"); checks whether the count of the old and updated list is the same, which is true for all add operations, and false for all other operations.

The PexAssert.IsTrue(Old_arrListContainsX, "Collection does not Contains the element"); and PexAssert.IsTrue(New_arrListContainsX, "Collection does not Contains the element after the operation"); statements verify whether the original collection contains the (presumably added) element (before and after the LastIndexOf operation).

Regarding the debugging: if your test fails and Visual Studio breaks into debug mode, it would be useful to look at Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount, Old_arrListIndexOfX (should be the same regardless of the operation), New_arrListIndexOfX and New_Ret. These will provide some clues as to why your tests are failing and may even help you spot what method is causing trouble.

I hope I've understood correctly what you are trying to achieve and this helps you to write your tests as expected.

Best, Michael

P.S You may need to replace int with whatever type your collection is. In general int is used as a placeholder for any specific type that the actual test will use.

Greetings 

Michael


A: Here's how you can implement the checks:

```csharp
private void MyArrayListCheck(ArrayList oldList, int x, int expectedIndex, ArrayList newList)
{
    if (expectedIndex >= 0)
    {
        //Count is same for both collections
        PexAssert.AreEqual(newList.Count, oldList.Count, "Count is unequal");

        //Contains method returns true after addition
        PexAssert.IsTrue(newList.Contains(x), "Contains method returned false after addition");

        //Element is at expected index
        PexAssert.AreEqual(expectedIndex, newList.IndexOf(x), "Element is not at the expected index");
    }
    else
    {
        //Check if element was not added (and therefore count remains same in both collections)
        PexAssert.AreEqual(newList.Count, oldList.Count, "Count is unequal");
        //If element was not in the list, it won't be in the new list either
        PexAssert.IsFalse(newList.Contains(x), "Contains method returned true after addition");
    }
}
```
You can use this method in your Pex method like this:

