```

This property should validate the behavior of the `LastIndexOf` method, particularly when the list contains the specified element `x`. The property assumes that the list is unchanged when it is created, except for the addition of one element. The element added might be the same as the element looked for, or it might be a new element not previously in the list.

## Conclusion
In this post, we've seen how to use Pex for generating unit tests for a `LastIndexOf` method. However, be aware that the approach described could be different depending on the complexity of the methods you are testing. Pex by Microsoft offers a very powerful library for automatic generation of suitable test cases for any method signature.


The above description assumes the method being tested is public and has a signature like `public int LastIndexOf(int item)`. You would need to modify the code according to your method's signature and make the appropriate changes in the tests. Do run the tests in your debug session before committing them, to avoid the unnecessary generation of thousands of tests and wasted computing resources.

Also, if your method has any side effects or if it does not satisfy Pre/Post conditions, these should also be taken into consideration during the test generation.

This post covered the basics of generating unit tests for methods using Pex. There is much more to discover as we continue exploring the capabilities of Pex, such as exploring the different ways of testing edge cases and more. Feel free to explore all the powerful methods Pex offers.

For additional info, you can refer to the official documentation on [Code Contracts](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/unit-test-your-code-with-visual-studio-code-coverage?view=vs-2019). 

Happy Testing!!

